## SECTION 1 — HEADER BANNER
<div class="header-banner">
    <h1>Lesson Planner – Theme # Go to the Window. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table class="lesson-table">
    <tr>
        <td><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        <td><b>Theme:</b> Go to the Window.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can draw and label classroom objects and their locations (e.g., drawing a book on a desk and writing 'book on the desk').
            Can engage in interactive labeling activities by labeling peer-created drawings of objects (e.g., labels a peer's sketch of a car with the word 'car').
            Can trace letters and short words related to locations to label objects (e.g., 'in' or 'on').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2>The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table class="aoa-table">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Stage & Description</th>
            <th>Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th>Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and leads a quick "Where Is It?" game using real classroom objects. Teacher holds up a familiar object (e.g., 'book', 'pencil') and asks "Where is the book?". Students point to where the book is in the classroom and try to say a preposition (e.g., "on," "under"). Teacher emphasizes /w/ in 'where' and /b/ in 'book'. Students are encouraged to "go to the window" or "go to the desk" as commands are given, activating TPR.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows large flashcards with classroom objects (chair, desk, book, pencil) and simple preposition words (in, on, under). The teacher models tracing a large letter 'K' on the board, then 'L', 'M', 'N', 'O'. Then, the teacher models tracing a short word like 'on' or 'in' with a finger on a card, saying the word clearly. Teacher demonstrates how to match a word card (e.g., 'book') to a picture of a book.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a book on a desk) "Is the book *on* the desk or *under* the desk?" (Students respond: "On!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a pencil) "Is this a *pen* or a *pencil*?" (Students respond: "Pencil!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a card with 'in') "Is this word 'in' or 'on'?" (Students respond: "In!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?")</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large picture chart titled "Our Classroom: Where Is It?" showing simple drawings of classroom objects (e.g., a yellow book on a blue desk, a red pencil under a chair, a green bag next to a table). Below each drawing, there's a simple, traceable label like 'book on the desk' or 'pencil under the chair'. The teacher points to each drawing and reads the label clearly, emphasizing the prepositions and object names.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions (e.g., 'The book is on the desk.'). Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions (e.g., 'Go to the window.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Point and Say:</b> Teacher says, "Point to the book *on* the desk." Students point to the correct drawing on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True or False (Visual Check):</b> Teacher asks, "Is the pencil *on* the chair?" (pointing to the drawing where it's *under*). Students respond "No!" or "Under!".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Circle the Preposition:</b> Teacher shows the chart again. "Is this word 'in' or 'on'?" Students point to the correct word if they can recognize it.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Tracing letters (K, L, M, N, O). Tracing short words ('in', 'on', 'under', 'book', 'desk', 'chair', 'pencil'). Matching words to pictures. Prepositions of place and classroom object nouns. Asking politely about location (e.g., 'Show me the blue crayon, please.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Tracing Letters and Words:</b> Students receive a worksheet with large dotted letters K, L, M, N, O, followed by dotted outlines of target words: 'in', 'on', 'under', 'book', 'desk', 'chair', 'pencil'. Students use crayons or markers to trace each letter and word carefully. The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance and praising efforts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Picture and Word Match-Up:</b> Students receive a second worksheet with simple drawings of classroom objects (e.g., a book, a chair, a pencil) on one side and a word bank of the corresponding words ('book', 'chair', 'pencil', 'desk') with prepositions ('in', 'on', 'under') on the other. Students draw a line from each picture to its correct word. For a more advanced challenge, they might match pictures of objects in specific locations (e.g., a book *on* a desk) to simple phrases (e.g., 'on the desk').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Labeling (Guided):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with 3-4 simple line drawings of objects in locations (e.g., a box *on* a table, a marker *in* a box, a ruler *under* a chair). Below each drawing, there is a space for a word or a phrase, possibly with the first letter or a dotted outline. Students try to write or trace the correct preposition (in, on, under) or the object name to complete the label. E.g., for "box on a table", they might trace 'on'. Teacher guides by asking, "Where is the marker?" and prompts "It's _in_ the box."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?")</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Labeled Drawing" activity. Each student receives a blank sheet of paper and drawing materials (crayons, markers). They are instructed to draw one or two simple classroom objects (e.g., a book, a pencil, a bag) and place them in a specific location relative to another object (e.g., a book *on* a desk, a pencil *under* a chair, a bag *next to* a table). After drawing, they try to write or trace a simple label for their drawing. This label could be just the object's name ('book') or the preposition ('on') or a short phrase ('book on'). The teacher provides a word bank on the board with pictures and traced versions of 'book', 'desk', 'chair', 'pencil', 'in', 'on', 'under' for reference.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students are physically demonstrating their understanding of "Where Is It?" by drawing and then attempting to use writing to convey this information. For example, a student draws a book on a desk and attempts to write 'book' and 'on' next to it, thus communicating the location.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in drawing and attempting to write short labels, applying the learned vocabulary and prepositions. Students share their drawings with a peer; the peer points to an object and asks, "Where is the book?" (if it's drawn) and the creator responds with a simple phrase or gesture, while the written label serves as the main output for writing skill focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the "My Labeled Drawing" activity, using a simple checklist to note students' attempts at drawing and labeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students display their "My Labeled Drawing" to the class or a small group. Each student briefly shows their drawing, points to an object, and says its name. The teacher checks if the student has attempted to write or trace an object name or a preposition on their drawing. For example, a student points to their drawing of a book and says "Book!", and the teacher observes if 'book' or 'on' (if it's on something) is written or traced next to it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can draw a recognizable classroom object (e.g., book, desk, chair).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to write or trace at least one object name (e.g., 'book', 'desk') correctly or intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to write or trace at least one preposition of place (e.g., 'in', 'on', 'under') correctly or intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates in showing and describing their drawing.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "Thumbs Up" if they think they can draw and write one word, or "Thumbs Down" if it was too hard. The teacher can ask, "Can you trace the word 'on'?" (Thumbs up/down). "Did you like drawing your picture?" (Thumbs up/down).</p>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did you trace or write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to write 'on'?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students engage with tracing and simple labeling? Were the instructions clear enough for Kinder learners?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific challenges did students face with letter formation or matching words to pictures? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., larger tracing guides, more tactile activities) are needed for future writing lessons?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will apply their knowledge of objects and locations, including their emerging writing skills, to create a collaborative classroom map or "Our Room" poster, labeling key items and their positions. This will integrate the drawing and labeling practiced today with a larger project.</p>
            </td>
            <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>